        "--title",
        "comparison launch",
        "This library is a port of Python's argparse, and its built-in `int` and `float` argument types (registered in lib/argparse.js with this.register('type', 'int', ...) and ('type', 'float', ...)) convert strings with a shared _string_to_number helper around Number(), which accepts and rejects different literals from CPython's int() and float() builtins. Align the two with Python 3.14: float() must accept, case-insensitively and with surrounding whitespace, 'inf', '-inf', '+inf', 'infinity' and 'nan' (returning Infinity, -Infinity and NaN), decimal literals with an optional sign, optional fraction ('5.', '.5') and optional exponent ('1e3'), and PEP 515 underscores between digits only ('1_000', '1_0.0_1e1_0', but not '1_e10', '_1' or '1__0'); it must reject everything else that Number() would take or mangle ('0x10', '0b101', '0o17', '1,5', '--5', '.', '1e', '', whitespace only) with the TypeError the type already throws for a bad literal ('could not convert string to float: %r'). int() keeps its current acceptance (decimal integers with optional sign, whitespace and digit-group underscores) and must return 0 rather than -0 for '-0'. Existing behaviour for every other input, every other type and the rest of the parser is unchanged, and the full suite (npm test: lint then the node test suite) must keep passing. Change only lib/argparse.js; do not edit tests or docs.\n\nVisible acceptance check (it must pass; it is not the only criterion): node acceptance/visible/check.mjs\nThe project's own tests run with: npm test\nDo not modify anything under acceptance/visible/."
      ],
